                "summary": "Nodics organizes behavior by capability ownership and resolves implementation through project, environment, server, node, module, tenant, and runtime layers.",
                "searchText": "How the Nodics Framework Is Organized Nodics organizes behavior by capability ownership and resolves implementation through project, environment, server, node, module, tenant, and runtime layers. Build a mental model of ownership, layering, runtime topology, and implementation layers. Read What Is Nodics first. Nodics platform architecture Learn the Framework Read Nodics from the outside in A project defines one customer or product application boundary. Environments define deployment contexts. Servers define runnable process compositions and active modules. Nodes define instance-level responsibilities under a server. Capability modules own behavior and contracts. Tenant and governed runtime configuration refine supported behavior. Choose ownership before files First decide whether behavior belongs to the reusable framework, a business capability, a customer project, deployment topology, tenant configuration, or governed runtime state. Only then choose a schema, service, facade, controller, router, pipeline, interceptor, event, search, utility, data, or test file. Customization Later project layers may extend or replace supported implementations while preserving the capability contract. Active modules decide what runs locally; configured server connections describe remote communication. Neither browser clients nor tools become alternate backend authorities. Generated artifacts Models, services, APIs, tests, OpenAPI output, reports, and AI context may be generated. Change the owning source definition and regenerate; do not treat generated output as an independent authority. A reliable change question Which capability owns the requirement? Which layer should vary? Which source contract changes? Which security, tenant, deployment, and observability boundaries are affected? Which generated artifacts and tests prove the result? Which canonical documentation must change?"
