                "searchText": "Libraries and Utilities Libraries and utilities provide small explicit mechanics; they never become hidden homes for business logic, provider selection, configuration loading, or runtime state. Decide whether reusable code belongs in a capability library, shared utility, or service. Understand layer ownership. Owning capability or nCommon for genuinely framework-wide primitives Platform Capabilities Placement rule Keep capability-specific helpers beside the owning capability. Use nCommon only for stable framework-wide deterministic primitives. Use a service when behavior needs configuration, I/O, state, policy, providers, or business meaning. Pass tenant, request, module, correlation, and execution context explicitly. Risks of casual sharing A shared helper becomes a widely consumed compatibility contract. Hidden I/O or globals make testing, override, security, and performance unpredictable. Moving code merely to shorten an import path erases ownership. A second utility, enum, error, or status registry creates parallel authority. Continue [Design and Extend Shared Helpers](/docs/capabilities/libraries-utilities/runtime) [Libraries and Utilities Technical Reference](/docs/reference/libraries-utilities)"
