                "summary": "Model business meaning first, declare only owned differences, and let Nodics create the runtime model and provider interaction.",
                "searchText": "Model a Business Entity Model business meaning first, declare only owned differences, and let Nodics create the runtime model and provider interaction. Add or extend a business entity using the supported schema framework. Know the owning capability, users, lifecycle, and relationships. The business module that owns the entity Build and Extend Example: employee contact information An Employee is profile-owned. Contact and Address records have their own fields and lifecycle and may be selected or created while editing an Employee. The employee schema declares governed references; the UI discovers metadata but the backend remains responsible for validation, authorization, and persistence. Implementation path Confirm an existing schema cannot be extended before adding a new one. Choose a stable code, owner, identity, tenant scope, required fields, defaults, and lifecycle. Declare relationships and access metadata using configuration values that layered projects can override. Enable search, cache, versioning, publishing, or workflow only where required. Use schema item models and services; never install or call a database client from the business module. Verification Create, read, update, search, deactivate, and delete behavior allowed by policy. Required, optional, default, invalid type, length, range, enum, and immutable fields. Existing-related-record selection and authorized nested creation. Invalid, missing, cross-tenant, cross-enterprise, and cyclic relationships. Provider-neutral contracts plus enabled cache, search, event, versioning, publishing, and workflow integration. Regression of normal schema update and unrelated schemas."
