                "depth": "overview",
                "summary": "Validation protects data shape and business invariants, while policy enforcement decides whether an authenticated principal may perform an operation in the resolved context.",
                "searchText": "Validation and Policy Enforcement Validation protects data shape and business invariants, while policy enforcement decides whether an authenticated principal may perform an operation in the resolved context. Understand how Nodics prevents invalid or unauthorized changes across every entry path. Read Schema and Item Modeling. Each capability owns its rules; Nodics validation, security, and pipeline layers enforce shared contracts Platform Capabilities Business value Apply the same rule to APIs, imports, jobs, workflows, BackOffice, and internal services. Reject invalid data before it corrupts authoritative records or derived indexes. Separate human login from service-to-service and scheduled-process authentication. Explain failures with stable codes while protecting sensitive details. Allow customer projects to add stricter rules through governed extension points. Validation is not authorization Validation asks whether the request and intended state are structurally and logically valid. Authentication establishes an identity. Authorization and access policy determine whether that identity may act on the target. A successful result in one layer never bypasses the others. Continue [Validation Layers and Policy Boundaries](/docs/capabilities/validation/layers) [Validation Runtime and Error Behavior](/docs/capabilities/validation/runtime) [Add a Business Validation Rule](/docs/capabilities/validation/tutorial) [Validation Technical Reference](/docs/reference/validation)"
