                "searchText": "Caching in Nodics Cache channels define tenant-safe keys, eligibility, TTL, invalidation, fallback, and provider requirements while authoritative data remains recoverable from its owner. Understand how Nodics improves response time without making cache authoritative. Understand the owning source of data. Nodics provider-neutral cache capability and configured cache adapters Platform Capabilities Business outcomes Reduce repeated database, search, API, configuration, and computation work. Select local or distributed behavior per channel and topology. Change TTL and provider through layered configuration. Fail safely when a provider cannot meet security or consistency requirements. Core rule A cache is a temporary projection. Every entry needs an owner, source fallback, bounded key, isolation scope, TTL, invalidation trigger, acceptable staleness, outage rule, diagnostics, and tests. Continue [Prebuilt Cache Providers and Maturity](/docs/capabilities/caching/prebuilt-providers) [Build a Cache Provider](/docs/capabilities/caching/provider-development) [Cache Lifecycle and Invalidation](/docs/capabilities/caching/lifecycle) [Operate and Secure Cache](/docs/capabilities/caching/operations) [Caching Technical Reference](/docs/reference/caching)"
