                "summary": "A provider maps normalized publish, subscribe, acknowledgement, error, retry, and lifecycle behavior to a broker while preserving Nodics event context.",
                "searchText": "Build a Messaging Provider A provider maps normalized publish, subscribe, acknowledgement, error, retry, and lifecycle behavior to a broker while preserving Nodics event context. Integrate a broker without moving transport logic into publishers or listeners. Know the provider-neutral EMS contract and target broker. Reusable messaging-provider or customer-project adapter module Build and Extend Implementation path Audit existing EMS adapters and delivery contracts. Create an owned provider module. Declare connection aliases, handlers, destinations, serializers, acknowledgement, retry, timeout, and capability flags in layered configuration. Implement connect, publish, subscribe, consume, acknowledge or reject, diagnostics, readiness, reconnect, and close as supported. Preserve tenant, source, target, node, trace, event type, version, headers, and sanitized failures. Keep listener business logic and NEMS persistence outside the broker adapter. Security Use governed destination aliases rather than request-supplied topics or queues. Keep credentials, certificates, endpoints, and broker topology in secret/deployment configuration. Validate payload type, size, schema/version, tenant, and allowed source/target. Do not log unrestricted payloads or use sensitive fields as telemetry labels. Proof Contract, configuration, serialization, and compatibility tests. Tenant isolation, authorization, idempotency, ordering, redelivery, retry, and poison-message behavior. Broker outage, reconnect, backpressure, partial failure, shutdown, and replay. Live security, capacity, recovery, failover, and upgrade qualification."
