            "searchText": "Job Lifecycle, Resilience, and Operations Create, update, run, start, stop, pause, resume, and remove operate on persisted definitions while runtime pools rebuild idempotently after restart. Configure, execute, restart, and recover jobs safely. Know the job owner, schedule, handler, and side effects. CronJob definitions, scheduler, lifecycle services, and owning handler Deploy and Operate Definition Stable code, tenant, schedule, active state, handler, start/end, priority, node assignment, timeout, retry, overlap, idempotency, retention, and permissions. Customer jobs belong in project modules; no arbitrary request service names, URLs, credentials, or executable handlers. Resilience Drain stops acquisition and process schedules while persisted definitions survive. Restart rebuilds eligible schedules idempotently. Node loss, network partition, and timeout can create uncertain outcomes; define duplicate policy, reconciliation, and compensation. Retry only safe or explicitly idempotent work; process-local flags are not distributed locks. Evidence Positive, invalid, unauthorized, cross-tenant, schedule boundary, manual run, overlap, timeout, retry, restart, drain, node loss/return, recovery, reconciliation, performance, integration, and regression tests.",
