            "sourceHash": "f67688f48cc5bdff5da4dc5e2237381aec9f7a7407710f7760f42d68c0c0f68d",
            "searchText": "Create and Operate Scheduled Jobs Define an approved persisted job, implement business work in its owning service, assign eligible node placement, secure lifecycle commands, and prove idempotency, overlap, retry, restart, and reconciliation. Create a safe tenant-owned scheduled job and operate it through restart, failure, and recovery. Understand services, service identity, tenant context, and runtime topology. CronJob owns scheduling and lifecycle; the selected handler or target module owns business behavior Platform Capabilities Example: reconcile stock reservations Suppose an ecommerce project must find expired stock reservations every five minutes and release them. Inventory owns reservation rules and updates. CronJob owns when the approved Inventory handler runs; it does not reimplement reservation logic. 1. Design the contract Choose a stable tenant-scoped job code, business owner, schedule and timezone, active state, start/end window, priority, normal runOnNode, and optional qualified failover policy. Name the approved owning handler or target-module operation; never accept arbitrary service names, URLs, credentials, node identifiers, or executable code from untrusted request data. Define timeout, maximum concurrency, overlap behavior, retry count/backoff, idempotency key, duplicate policy, retention, quarantine, reconciliation, compensation, and alert thresholds. Decide whether a workflow or event is more appropriate: use Workflow for durable multi-step/manual business processes and events for decoupled facts; do not hide those responsibilities in a cron handler. 2. Implement in the owning project module Contribute the job definition and configuration through a later project module so framework CronJob remains reusable. Implement reservation discovery and release in Inventory services using its schema models, validation, authorization, and persistence contracts. Keep the scheduled handler bounded and resumable; process records in pages and persist enough progress or idempotency state to recover safely. When the job calls another module, use the tenant-scoped internal service-token flow and satisfy that module's route permission. Never store or reuse an employee username/password. 3. Register and run Import or create the definition through the governed CronJob contracts; startup side effects must not create duplicates. An eligible CronJob module instance builds its disposable local schedule from the persisted definition when runOnNode or approved tempNode matches. Scheduled and authorized manual runs use the same definition, handler, tenant, eligibility, overlap, evidence, and failure contracts. A server only hosts active modules; CronJob remains module-owned regardless of consolidated or distributed process placement. 4. Operate and recover Use secured create, update, run, start, stop, pause, resume, and remove commands; state changes must not use GET. Monitor due, started, completed, failed, skipped, schedule delay, duration, overlap denial, attempts, node assignment/handoff, downstream latency, stale running state, and reconciliation outcome. Graceful drain stops acquisition and local schedules while the persisted definition survives; restart rebuilds eligible schedules idempotently. A timeout, network partition, crash, or node loss may leave the business outcome unknown. Reconcile authoritative Inventory state before retrying or compensating. Verification Positive schedule and manual run; invalid definition; inactive job; unauthorized, wrong-token-type, and cross-tenant access. Start/end and timezone boundaries; duplicate definition; concurrent trigger; overlap denial; timeout; safe and unsafe retry; pagination; rate/capacity limits. Restart, drain, hard termination, node loss/return, delayed failure detection, downstream outage/recovery, temporary ownership, idempotency, reconciliation, and compensation. Contract, integration, modular topology, observability/redaction, performance, upgrade, and regression evidence.",
