                "searchText": "Workflow Lifecycle, Extension, and Recovery Definitions select a head action; commands or events create tenant-scoped carriers; transitions persist before continuation; restart recovers from durable state. Design and operate a durable workflow safely. Know the business states, actors, items, decisions, and side effects. Workflow definitions, durable carriers/items, action/channel services, and target capabilities Platform Capabilities Lifecycle Initialize carrier/items from governed mappings. Release begins automatic work or exposes a permitted manual action. Action result selects an allowed channel and persists the transition. Splits create traceable branches; aggregation waits under explicit policy. Pause, resume, block, retry, cancel, finish, archive, and error states remain durable. Extension Project modules add definitions, mappings, actions, channels, handlers, interceptors, pipelines, events, validation, and access policy. Runtime workflow-builder data must use the same governed definitions and cannot accept arbitrary executable code. External API and AI actions use adapters, secrets, token/cost policy, timeouts, idempotency, audit, and human approval where risk requires. Recovery proof Duplicate/out-of-order events, concurrency, restart, remote timeout, late response, retry/replay, split/aggregation, definition versions, cancellation, compensation, redaction, performance, integration, and regression."
