                "summary": "Nodics customization uses layered project modules and governed runtime configuration so different customer projects can change the same reusable capability independently.",
                "searchText": "Customize and Extend Nodics Nodics customization uses layered project modules and governed runtime configuration so different customer projects can change the same reusable capability independently. Customize a customer project without forking or creating a competing authority. Understand modules, active load order, configuration, schemas, services, and runtime governance. Project modules loaded after reusable framework/domain modules Build and Extend Choose the smallest extension Configuration: change an exposed property in project/environment/server/node or governed runtime configuration. Data: import project-owned reference, core, or sample data through Nodics import contracts. Layer override: contribute a later schema, service, facade, controller, route, pipeline, interceptor, status, or utility definition using the established loader. Provider: implement the provider-neutral adapter contract when integrating a new database, cache, search, event, AI, storage, identity, notification, or payment technology. New capability: create a project module only when no existing authority can be reused or safely extended. Rules Never edit released reusable modules for customer-only policy. Do not introduce a second loader, registry, configuration source, persistence path, workflow engine, or governance layer. Keep application values out of package.json; keep secrets outside committed properties and data. Do not place helper constants inside schema definitions when a named configurable contribution must remain overrideable. Preserve stable public contracts or version them deliberately; document override precedence and rollback. Prove customization Verify the base project remains unchanged, the custom project receives only its intended override, and another project can apply a different override. Test load order, absent/invalid configuration, runtime activation, audit/rollback where supported, topology boundaries, upgrade compatibility, and regression. Update the canonical customization guide and owning module README with the supported extension point."
