                "searchText": "Test Nodics Changes Nodics validation proves successful behavior and rejected behavior, boundaries, contracts, integrations, resilience, security, performance, topology, generated artifacts, and regressions. Select proportional tests that prove a Nodics change across layers, authorities, and deployment topologies. Understand the capability owner, public contracts, configuration, security, and runtime topology. Owning module tests plus repository-wide Nodics governance and regression suites Build and Extend Required dimensions Positive: the intended authorized workflow succeeds. Negative: invalid, missing, unauthorized, wrong-principal, and wrong-tenant requests fail safely. Boundary: empty, maximum, transition, concurrency, timeout, retry, replay, pagination, and size limits. Contract: schemas, routes, statuses, events, provider interfaces, generated artifacts, and backward compatibility. Integration: real module boundaries and qualified local providers where feasible; mocks alone are not completion evidence. Regression: existing capabilities, base modules, custom layers, imports, documentation, and generated context remain valid. Topology and resilience Use MonoServer for fast local integration while functionality is emerging. Verify relevant modules independently in modular topology before production readiness; module ownership matters more than a fixed server list. Test startup ordering, dependency absence, asynchronous registration, stale leases, restart, provider outage, partial failure, retry/backoff, idempotency, recovery, graceful shutdown, and observability. For staged/online publishing, test separate systems or schemas, approval boundaries, publication, cache invalidation, failure recovery, and online non-versioned behavior. Security and release evidence Test human login separately from module/service and cron credentials. Prove tenant/enterprise isolation, least privilege, secrets handling, response masking, audit, abuse controls, and browser protections where applicable. Run focused tests first, then repository governance and relevant baseline suites; regenerate derived documentation/context before its validators. Record exact commands, results, limitations, environment assumptions, and untested production dependencies."
