                "depth": "implementation",
                "summary": "Authentication evidence never grants universal access; each target operation enforces token class, permission, tenant, schema action, field visibility, ownership, and business policy.",
                "searchText": "Authorization and Ownership Authentication evidence never grants universal access; each target operation enforces token class, permission, tenant, schema action, field visibility, ownership, and business policy. Understand route, group, permission, schema, field, and record-ownership enforcement. Understand authenticated principal types. Each target module owns route and data authorization; identity governance owns principal/group integrity Secure and Govern Policy layers User groups compose catalogued permissions through active acyclic inheritance. Routes enforce required token class, permissions, and API exposure. Schemas define action/group access, field masking, and ownership policy. Customer-owned records constrain ownerId; explicit administrative and trusted-service groups may bypass only according to layered policy. Cross-tenant internal-token access requires a separate permission. Extension Reusable policy names and mappings belong in properties.js, not closure constants inside schemas. Projects may add stricter layered policy but must not silently weaken framework security. Axis discovers allowed contracts and renders controls; backend services remain authoritative. Nested creation and related-record selection invoke each related schema's permissions and validation. Verification Allowed and denied action/group/permission combinations. Field read/write masking and immutable values. Own versus another principal's records. Inactive, missing, cyclic, or inherited groups. Human, customer, service, scheduled, same-tenant, and cross-tenant boundaries."
