                "summary": "Profile owns tenants, enterprises, people, employees, customers, service principals, groups, credentials, contacts, addresses, and authentication state; other modules reference these contracts without duplicating them.",
                "searchText": "Tenants, Enterprises, and Identity Models Profile owns tenants, enterprises, people, employees, customers, service principals, groups, credentials, contacts, addresses, and authentication state; other modules reference these contracts without duplicating them. Model organizational context and identity relationships safely. Understand schema relationships and authorization. Profile identity and tenancy system of record Secure and Govern Context model Tenant is the isolation and configuration scope selected before identity and business operations. Enterprise represents the governed business organization within the supported tenant model. Parent enterprise relationships express hierarchy but must use user-friendly labels such as Parent Enterprise and Enterprise. Employee and customer are distinct human roles; service principals represent non-human callers. Contacts and addresses have independent governed relationships and may be selected or created when policy allows. Propagation APIs resolve enterprise and tenant before target authorization. Jobs, imports, events, workflows, assistant tools, cache keys, database models, search models, and audit preserve the same context. A caller may narrow but never broaden trusted tenant scope. Cross-module relationships invoke the target module contract; no direct collection writes. Isolation proof Same codes in different tenants. Cross-tenant token, query, relationship, cache, event, import, and export denial. Parent/child enterprise cycles and inactive relationships. Nested related creation authorization and rollback. Tenant-safe diagnostics, backups, indexes, and operational dimensions."
