            "sourceHash": "cbcfa0d803a405ed7aef8ebbe13894ffbc41f16d2d7c053923cae6386f6d675e",
            "searchText": "Run and Debug Nodics Use MonoServer for fast local integration and modular servers for boundary validation; select topology through metadata-driven environment, server, and node inputs rather than long encoded names. Start the intended local topology, understand what loaded, and diagnose startup or runtime failures safely. Complete local setup and understand modules and environments. nConfig startup and lifecycle contracts plus the selected project environment, server, and node modules Deploy and Operate Choose a topology MonoServer loads the configured local module composition into one process for fast development, debugging, core import, and early end-to-end tests. Modular servers run selected module compositions as separate processes so HTTP, authentication, registration, provider, timeout, outage, restart, and lifecycle boundaries can be tested. Server names may repeat across environments because identity is derived from validated ancestry; supply ENV explicitly for deterministic scripts and automation. A node is an optional instance-specific child of a server. Starting a node requires its server; environment may be inferred only when that server is unique. Start explicitly For deterministic local execution use the selected metadata names, for example `ENV=startioLocal SERVER=monoServer npm run start`. For a node use `ENV=startioLocal SERVER=cronServer NODE=cronNode0 npm run start`. If an interactive terminal supplies an ambiguous server without ENV, Nodics offers the discovered environments for selection. Non-interactive CI, containers, and services fail with candidate environments and an explicit command example instead of waiting for a prompt. activeModules decides what loads locally; servers.* provides endpoint coordinates and never activates a remote module in the current process. Local BackOffice topology Begin Axis integration against MonoServer while capabilities are changing rapidly. Before considering the backend boundaries complete, run at least Profile, CMS staged, CMS online, CronJob, NEMS, DEAP, and BackOffice in their relevant modular processes. CMS staged and online use distinct systems or schemas; staged authoring/versioning and approved publication must not collapse into one data authority. A module registers with BackOffice asynchronously; registration failure must not add synchronous latency to unrelated request traffic. Troubleshoot Confirm the selected environment/server/node, active module list, deterministic index order, parent metadata, effective properties, and servers.* coordinates. Check mandatory dependency readiness, provider activation, database/search/cache connectivity, bootstrap identity, ports, route exposure, and safe status codes. Use correlation identifiers and redacted logs; never print passwords, API keys, bearer/refresh tokens, cookies, private keys, or connection credentials. Stop processes through the lifecycle coordinator so draining and provider cleanup run; hard termination cannot guarantee hooks. Record exact topology, command, effective non-secret configuration, failure, and focused test evidence when reporting a defect.",
