            "searchText": "Module Implementation Reference A Nodics module is a package-defined ownership boundary with canonical metadata, layered configuration, loader-visible source contributions, owned data/tests/docs, and later-layer customization—not merely a folder or server. Create, inspect, extend, and validate a Nodics module using the standard ownership and loader contracts. Understand layered architecture, configuration, schemas, services, APIs, testing, and documentation. nConfig module metadata/loading and the concrete module that owns each contribution Reference Required root contract package.json: standard package identity/dependencies plus the minimal package.json.nodics metadata contract. nodics.js: module lifecycle entrypoint used by Nodics startup/loading. AGENTS.md: local AI/developer behavior and ownership rules. README.md: concise human purpose, ownership, configuration, extension, operations, verification, and canonical-documentation links. config/properties.js, prescripts.js, and postscripts.js: mandatory layered extension points, even when intentionally empty. llm/README.md and generated module context for module-shaped packages except the designated setup authority. Metadata package.json.name is the canonical runtime identifier; established identifiers require explicit compatibility migration before rename. package.json.nodics.kind classifies application, group, environment, server, node, module, or other supported boundary; never infer kind from the folder name or n-prefix. package.json.nodics.runtime and runtimeModule are the runtime/loader flags; package.json.nodics.owns declares owned source surfaces. Parent, required, index, dependency, and composition metadata establish deterministic hierarchy and load order. Runtime policy, permissions, navigation, endpoints, provider selection, limits, feature switches, and BackOffice presentation belong in layered properties.js, not package.json. Do not introduce a parallel manifest or duplicate metadata name. Source ownership src/schemas/schemas.js: item definitions, relationships, access, generation, cache/event/transaction, persistence, and search alignment. src/router/routers.js and appConfig.js: route and HTTP application contributions. src/controller: transport mapping only; src/facade: orchestration; src/service: business/provider/tooling behavior. src/pipelines/pipelines.js: pipeline registry; src/event/listeners.js: listener definitions; src/interceptors: validation/lifecycle interception. src/search/indexes.js: owned search indexes; src/utils: mergeable utilities, enums, and status definitions; src/lib: class-based libraries. data/init is mandatory bootstrap only; data/core and data/sample are explicit imports. Pure groups and project roots do not own empty data folders. test contains behavior owned by that layer. docs contains permanent local technical references and is excluded from runtime module discovery. Implementation and customization Search existing authorities and extension points first; reuse, then customize, and create new behavior last. Keep runtime files inside loader radar and export mergeable members so later modules can replace one behavior without copying the whole file. Use named configurable policies instead of closure constants or hardcoded project roles/servers/tenants/providers. Generated blank defaultSampleService.js is an optional module-creation placeholder only. It must remain empty and is never a required service or business-logic owner. Do not hand-edit generated controllers, facades, services, models, tests, documentation projections, or context when their source definition/generator owns them. Keep customer business logic in customer project modules and frontend renderers in client repositories. Definition of done Metadata, parent/index, dependencies, ownership, configuration defaults/overrides, source map, permissions, tenancy, lifecycle, observability, performance, migration, deployment, and failure/recovery are explicit. Positive, negative, boundary, contract, integration, customization/override, topology, security, performance, upgrade, generated-artifact, and regression tests pass as applicable. Canonical user/developer documentation and concise module README match implementation; AGENTS and generated context express the same ownership rules. Package and application dependencies remain synchronized with the root dependency-governance contract. Structure, metadata, copyright, documentation, LLM/context, focused, baseline, and release gates report exact evidence and limitations.",
            "packVersion": "0.3.2",
